# Architectural inheritance

Shekyl forked from Monero (which forked from CryptoNote). Code, data
models, threat-model assumptions, and architectural choices were
inherited along with the fork. Most of that inheritance is load-bearing.
Some of it contradicts Shekyl's threat model.

## Scope

Pre-genesis, this rule applies to all Shekyl code. Inherited code is not
grandfathered. Post-genesis, new architectural decisions stay under this
rule; decisions already made under it change only by the amendment process.

## The rule

Inheriting code is not inheriting architecture. Code is text. Architecture
is the property the code delivers against the threat model. When inherited
code contradicts `00-mission.mdc`, `30-cryptography.mdc`,
`35-secure-memory.mdc`, or `36-secret-locality.mdc`, the architecture is
wrong for Shekyl however well it was tested in its original context.

Migrate the data model. Do not rationalize the inheritance.

`36-secret-locality.mdc` says Rust owns secrets at the C++/Rust boundary.
This rule asks which Rust component owns them. Orchestrator-persisted
derived per-output secrets satisfy rule 36 and still contradict engine
isolation. Both rules apply.

## A claim about the code is not the code

An inherited claim — upstream docs, a comment, a design doc we ratified —
is a hypothesis until someone reads the implementation. The failure that
matters is a claim true of the design and false of the implementation.

When a claim is load-bearing for a security property:

1. Read it at the implementation before relying on it, including Shekyl's
   own ratified documents. Provenance is not derivation
   (`05-system-thinking.mdc`). A seal is not coverage.
2. Give it a gate that can fail (`50-testing.mdc`,
   `47-gate-subject-assertion.mdc`): a derivation check, a pinned vector,
   an assertion that the constant equals the maximum it claims to be. A
   defence that cannot fail consumes the attention that would have found
   the gap.
3. Otherwise demote it to a recorded assumption and name the check owed.
   An unverifiable claim may be carried. It may not be carried as a property.

A claim whose violation fails loudly (an unknown enum arm that throws) is
a signpost. One that fails silently (a constant that stays valid-looking
while the world moves past it) is the hazard.

Four misreads recur. Each admits the convenient half of a claim and
inherits the half that carries the verdict.

- **Mood.** A present-tense sentence records a state. It is not a ruling.
  Before treating "the verifier does not use the dataset" as a prohibition,
  find the decision: a ratified question, a dated ruling, a reversion
  clause. Unbuilt-because-unmeasured is not forbidden.
- **Guard.** A conditional check is not evidence that the behaviour it
  guards is conditional. A nettype gate says nothing about whether the
  behaviour varies by nettype. Show the behaviour varies.
- **Direction.** An enumeration of what a component consumes says nothing
  about what its consumers must read back out of it. Name which direction
  the sweep ran.
- **Grouping.** A grouping someone else drew is a claim about its members.
  Test each member against the rule. Do not admit one on the strength of
  its neighbour.

## When to migrate, keep, or delete

- **Migrate** when inherited architecture contradicts a Shekyl security
  claim. Pre-genesis the cost is bounded. Post-genesis it is forever.
- **Keep** when the architecture fits the threat model and migrating would
  touch many sites without proportional benefit.
- **Document** the disposition. Every inherited subsystem has a recorded
  why: load-bearing, deletion target, or pending migration
  (`05-system-thinking.mdc`).

## Pre-genesis

Per `15-deletion-and-debt.mdc`, the pre-launch migration path is
`rm -rf ~/.shekyl` and re-sync. Structural work lands before genesis.
"We'll fix it in V3.x" is not a disposition.

## Cost-benefit-defer-to-later

For security-load-bearing structure, the cost of the broken shape
compounds with every layer that depends on it. When a review offers "fix
structurally now, or work around now and fix later," fix structurally now
unless the cost is genuinely prohibitive: multi-quarter scope, or
cross-component coordination that breaks the discipline budget.

## User-protection defaults in user-absent contexts

Production defaults exist to protect deployed users: graceful degradation,
staged rollouts, compatibility preservation, opt-in migration, soft
deprecation, version-skew tolerance. Pre-genesis there are no such users.
Those defaults invert: loud failure, atomic cutover, delete rather than
gate (`60-no-monero-legacy.mdc`), `rm -rf ~/.shekyl`, no
`[[deprecated]]` without a deletion version (`15-deletion-and-debt.mdc`),
no version dispatch.

When a design "gracefully accepts," "preserves compatibility,"
"soft-deprecates," or "tolerates skew," ask whether any pre-genesis user
is protected by that default. If none, the inversion is the disposition.
The same default re-enters every review under a new name (a new RPC
field, an FFI parameter, an error code). Ask each time.

## A comment that outlived its architecture

A comment that answers "why not X" with a mechanism becomes an instruction
to rebuild what was deleted. Principles survive re-architecture. Mechanisms
do not. When the mechanism is gone, the comment's conclusion is
unsupported even if it still happens to be right.

The tell: the comment prescribes a step the current architecture cannot
run, and following it reopens the hazard the architecture closed.

1. When a slice deletes or relocates a mechanism, the comments that reason
   from it are part of the deletion surface.
2. Record which premise failed. "Premise refuted" closes the question.
   "Superseded" invites re-litigation.
3. Enumerate the callers in the same slice. A surviving caller is the
   hazard the comment invited.
4. Write the constraint where the replacement's author will read it. A
   deletion in disposable code does not carry the constraint forward.

## Audits-are-clean-so-compress

Clean inheritance audits are the result of running the check. They are
not a reason to skip the next one. The reopening criterion for relaxing
the check is a substrate change that makes it no longer load-bearing, not
a run of clean results.

## Where to look

Weight the check by how close the surface is to Shekyl-specific secrets
and cryptography. Key and signing surfaces carry the findings. Data-flow
surfaces carry fewer. Pure utility surfaces usually carry none. A design
that was written for Shekyl (secrets in session state, public material on
the message path) passes by construction. Inherited CryptoNote architecture
needs the audit.

